Myth: In the first hush, the sky was a sealed clay bowl, and the earth beneath it dreamed in gray. The rivers lay stiff as sleeping snakes, the flowers were folded thoughts, and no creature knew the shape of morning.

At the root of the world stood Eron, the old tree, bare and patient. Under his roots lived Mera, the first toad, whose back was speckled with tiny dark moons. She heard a warmth beating far below, like a heart trapped in stone.

“Eron,” croaked Mera, “why do your branches not bloom?”

“I have no light to teach them,” Eron answered.

Mera puffed her throat and sang into the soil. Her song was rough, but it shook the hidden fire awake. For three nights she sang, until sparks rose through Eron’s roots and filled his trunk with gold.

On the fourth dawnless day, Eron stretched his branches and struck the clay bowl of the sky. It cracked. Through the crack poured sunlight, and wherever it touched Mera’s song, reeds lifted, rivers loosened, and flowers opened their bright mouths.

The sparks that missed the sky settled on Mera’s back, becoming her speckles.

That is why toads call from wet earth after storms, and why old trees bloom when light returns.
